1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,568
How many ways into the locked room should there be?

2
00:00:02,618 --> 00:00:05,064
The room has two kinds of visitor.

3
00:00:05,114 --> 00:00:08,904
Buyers' browsers come from the internet, to type a card into the frame.

4
00:00:08,954 --> 00:00:13,368
And services from other units, like ecom, come from their own servers.

5
00:00:13,418 --> 00:00:21,240
One big entrance that opened every visitor's traffic would see everything in clear text, and would be the most valuable box in the bank.

6
00:00:21,290 --> 00:00:24,888
So the room has two narrow doors, and nothing else listens.

7
00:00:24,938 --> 00:00:27,408
Think of a bank counter and a mail slot.

8
00:00:27,458 --> 00:00:29,640
The payers' door is the counter.

9
00:00:29,690 --> 00:00:37,944
It opens the browser's encrypted connection, passes on only the card frame's few requests, and answers not found to everything else.

10
00:00:37,994 --> 00:00:41,688
It also limits how fast one buyer can submit cards.

11
00:00:41,738 --> 00:00:44,064
The callers' door is the mail slot.

12
00:00:44,114 --> 00:00:49,296
A caller's connection arrives sealed, with the name of the service it wants written on the outside.

13
00:00:49,346 --> 00:00:55,104
The door reads only that name and passes the envelope on, still sealed: pass-through.

14
00:00:55,154 --> 00:00:59,736
Only a few names pass, and the service behind the door checks the caller itself.

15
00:00:59,786 --> 00:01:04,248
So no door ever holds every caller's traffic in clear text.

16
00:01:04,298 --> 00:01:06,888
Next: how a caller gets its certificate.
